---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Design from a Screenshot - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="design-from-screenshot">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="You can pass an image straight to the agent and have it build what it shows.">
			The design team wants a new section on the website's coverage page. There's no spec and no design file, only two
			screenshots.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message
			kind="email"
			subject="Add-ons section for /coverage"
			from="Design Team"
			to="Web Team"
			date="Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:40"
			attachments={[
				{ name: "coverage-add-ons-desktop.png", href: "/downloads/kaiju-mutual/3.coverage-add-ons-desktop.png" },
				{ name: "coverage-add-ons-phone.png", href: "/downloads/kaiju-mutual/3.coverage-add-ons-phone.png" },
			]}
		>
			<p>
				Hi team, can we get an "Optional add-ons" section on the coverage page, between the limits table and "What isn't
				covered"? Screenshots attached for desktop and phone.
			</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent build the section in the screenshots on the coverage page.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Put the section on /coverage, between the limits table and \"What isn't covered\".",
				"Match the screenshots: the heading and intro, the four add-on cards, the featured card and its badge, and the callout under the cards.",
				"Copy every price and line of text exactly as the screenshot shows it.",
				"Lay the cards out for desktop, tablet and phone, the way the screenshots show.",
				"Reuse the styles and patterns the website already has instead of adding new ones.",
				"Check the result in the browser at desktop and phone widths.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`Add the section in these screenshots to the website's coverage page (/coverage), between the limits table and "What isn't covered": <desktop image> <phone image>

Match the screenshots closely. Reuse the site's existing colors, section heading pattern and the featured plan styling from the home page rather than adding new styles, and put the copy with the rest of the site's copy. Check it in agent-browser at 1440 and 390 wide and compare against the screenshots.`}
		>
			<p>
				The screenshots don't say how the site is built. The codebase does: a good agent reuses the <code>section__head</code>{" "}
				pattern from the home page, styles the featured card like the home page's featured plan, uses the existing color
				tokens, and puts the add-on copy in <code>src/lib/content</code> with the rest of the copy. Check for each of those.
			</p>
			<p>
				The agent types the copy in by reading the image, so check the prices. A misread digit is easy to miss.
			</p>
			<p>
				Nothing of the section exists in the code or in git history, so the agent can't copy it from anywhere. If it says it
				found it, ask where.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
